  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 358 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday Night...\n\nThe overall forecast has remained very consistent with the heat \npeaking for the middle of the week. Guidance continues to show the \nlarge 594 mid-level ridge building over the Ohio Valley over the \nnext couple of days. This will keep our weather mainly dry, with \ndaily highs gradually increasing into the upper 90s with a few \nlocations possibly reaching the triple digits by Wednesday or \nThursday. Along with the increased in heat, we will see dew points \nclimb into the upper 60s with a few 70s but we still anticipate dry \nair mixing each afternoon and even some scattered mid-level Cu to \nalso develop. Heat index values will start to get into the low 100s \nthen near and above Heat Advisory criteria for Wednesday and \nThursday where confidence is growing we will likely need a heat \nheadline for Tuesday into Wednesday. \n\nThinking also remains that the hottest temperatures will be in areas \nalong and west of the I-65 corridor with slightly lower temperatures \nas you work east to northeast in the CWA into the Bluegrass and I-75 \ncorridors. LREF heat index probabilities have been pretty consistent \nrun to run with the probabilities of exceeding 100F at between 40-\n60% on Wednesday and 50-70% on Thursday along and west of I-65 with \nlower values as you go east and northeast. \n\nWhat elevates the heat risk with this current heatwave is not only \nthe duration of several days but the lack of relief of cooling \novernight. Lows will be in the low/mid 70s for most location with \nmany urban areas only dropping into the upper 70s to near 80 \novernight.\n\nFriday through Sunday...\n\nMoving into the late week and into the weekend, the upper air \npattern will start to change slightly.  A large upper trough/closed \nlow is forecast to plow into the Pacific northwest while the mid-\nlevel ridge over the OV/SE US starts to flatten out and retrograde \nslightly to the west.  If this were to occur, our flow will become \nnorthwesterly and we'll have to watch for perturbations riding \naround the ridge which may bring MCSs back into the forecast.  A \nfair amount of spread remains in the long range ensembles here \nindicating that not all the members are suggesting the ridge \nretrograding.  The OP Euro trends have been stronger with the ridge \nretrograding and bringing some relief to the region.  For now, 20-\n30% POPs out in the Fri-Sun period look reasonable at this time. The \ntropics will become more active in this time period and may end up \ninfluencing the pattern than other synoptic scale features.  As such, \nthe forecast confidence here is lower than average.  For now, we'll \nlikely see low-mid 90s on Friday for highs, but things may cool back \ninto the upper 80s/lower 90s for Sat/Sun, especially if we get \nconvection back into the forecast.",
